cs: release a held GFX clock/voltage override across a display modeset

On a BC-250 driving HDMI through an active DP->HDMI adapter, a *held* forced
GFX clock/voltage override makes the adapter's downstream link bring-up fail.
The DP side trains fine and reports success, so DC never sees a fault -- the
screen simply goes dark on a mode change, typically returning from a desktop
session to gamescope, and sometimes on the first gamescope start after boot.

Not specific to DSC. It reproduces with amdgpu.bc250_hdmi21=0, on an
uncompressed 4-lane HBR2 link, with no display patches of ours applied at all.

Two things are needed, and both were established by hardware A/B:

1) The override must come off around the modeset. Toggling cs_od_unforce_ms
   within a single boot, governor active and holding the override in both arms:
   3000 -> KDE<->gamescope survives, 0 -> signal lost, 3000 -> survives again.
   Deferring OD commits is NOT sufficient; observed failures had no commit
   anywhere near them. It is the held state that breaks the link.

2) The release must genuinely un-force the clock. RequestGfxclk (0xE) is a
   manual request with no "stop requesting" counterpart, so releasing by
   requesting the firmware's default clock through it leaves the clock under
   manual control. A build identical to this one except for that still lost the
   display on a mode change, with the release firing correctly every time.
   ForceGfxFreq (0x39) and UnForceGfxFreq (0x3A) are a matched pair, so the
   driver forces with 0x39 and releases with 0x3A. 0x3A is declared in
   smu_v11_8_ppsmc.h but was never added to the message map.

Commits arriving during the release window are refused with -EBUSY; their
settings are still recorded, and the restore applies them when the window
closes, so nothing is lost -- it is just applied a few seconds later.

The window is amdgpu.cs_od_unforce_ms, default 3000 ms, 0 disables the release
entirely and restores the behaviour of a kernel without this patch.

The modeset gate matters: amdgpu_dm_atomic_commit_tail() runs on every atomic
commit including every page flip, and an ungated call re-arms the restore timer
every frame so it never fires, locking the governor out of the GPU.
